        "original_id": "33800a52-d649-5044-a18b-e94ee7645405",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Bolesław Prus, Otto von Bismarck\n- Works: The Outpost (Placówka)\n- Places: Poland\n- Topics: rural poverty, German colonization, partitions of Poland\n\nFacts:\n- Otto von Bismarck was born on 1 April 1815 and died on 30 July 1898.\n- Otto von Bismarck was a conservative Prussian statesman who dominated German and European affairs from the 1860s until 1890.\n- Poland had been partitioned about a century earlier by Russia, Prussia, and Austria.\n- Bolesław Prus was a Polish writer.\n- The Outpost, titled Placówka in Polish, was the first of Bolesław Prus's four major novels.\n- In The Outpost, Bolesław Prus sought to draw attention to the plight of rural Poland.\n- Rural Poland had to contend with poverty, ignorance, neglect by the country's upper crust, and colonization by German settlers backed by Otto von Bismarck's German government.",
